├── .env.example
├── pytest.ini
└── requirements.txt
```

## Getting started

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate        # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
cp .env.example .env             # Windows: copy .env.example .env
```

## Train the model

```bash
python -m app.train
```

```
Accuracy: 0.933

              precision    recall  f1-score   support
...
Model saved to models/model.joblib
```

## Make a prediction

```bash
python -m app.predict 5.1 3.5 1.4 0.2
# Prediction: setosa (confidence 0.981)
```

Running `python -m app.predict` without arguments uses an example sample.

## Run the tests

```bash
pytest
```

## Using your own data

1. Put your dataset in `data/`.
2. Rewrite `load_dataset()` in `app/train.py` to read it; it only has to return
   features, labels, class names and feature names.
3. Adjust `build_pipeline()` with the preprocessing and estimator you need.
4. Run `python -m app.train` again.

Settings such as the model path, test split and random seed live in `.env`.
